      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Data from the FBI's Uniform Crime Reporting (UCR) and National Incident-Based Reporting System (NIBRS) consistently show that while Hispanic/Spanish origin groups have higher homicide rates per 100,000 people compared to Whites, they comprise a smaller percentage of the U.S. population (approx. 18.7% in 2022 vs. 62.1% for Whites). Regarding violent offenses like assaults and robberies, the Black population records the highest rates per capita (approx. 1,429 for male assaults in 2022), while White populations still represent the largest absolute numbers due to their demographic weighting. It is critical to note that these disparities often correlate strongly with socioeconomic variables, residential segregation, and policing intensity rather than inherent group differences, making aggregate raw numbers a precarious indicator of individual risk without adjusting for context.",
